Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Hawser Chews the Dunes
- Story & Context: At the dry-dock depot of Surajpur, caravan crews spent three days dragging a container of salvaged tone-generators through a salt basin using rusted ship-mooring cables that moaned under the desert wind. When the main winch cable snapped and whipped through a bank of running dynamos, it sent a shower of sparks across Suraj’s low-tuned congas, inspiring the opening rhythm of this track. Between the choking smoke and the smell of ozone, singer Kavita barked into an unshielded radio transmitter, shouting: "Bind the copper, brace the iron, let the hawser snap!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro shot of a frayed, tension-stretched steel harbor cable biting deep into cracked desert salt, throwing off electric sparks against a background of brutalist corrugated iron, high-contrast harsh lighting, gritty industrial texture.

Track 2: Kavita of the Deadwater Slipway
- Story & Context: Hidden inside an abandoned irrigation pumping station, Kavita spent forty-eight hours repairing a heat-damaged transistor board while listening to the phantom rhythmic sloshing of stagnant runoff under the concrete floor. The song is a bleak, rhythmic confession of trade-route exhaustion, propelled by a conga pattern played entirely with the knuckles against dry goat hide. Her voice cuts through an unstable, detuned bass synthesizer as she delivers the track’s ruthless centerpiece hook: "You trade for wire, you pay in marrow, but the deadwater will not lift your keel."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Silhouette of a lone female engineer hunched over a glowing soldering iron on a concrete slipway inside a vast, cavernous brutalist irrigation chamber, warm orange solder sparks contrasting against cold slate-grey shadows.

Track 3: Neap-Tide Bole
- Story & Context: According to caravan folklore, there exists an hour in the dry season when the wind reverses and the dried-up sea basin groans like an empty cargo bay waiting for water that never arrives. The Nakhudas tracked this seasonal shift by recording live conga hits bouncing off the canyon walls of an empty canal, layering them under a broken tumbi plugged directly into a blown horn speaker. The song culminates in a massive, sand-gritted chaal drop when the whole caravan bellows the hook into the dust storm: "Bole ni, the harbor is bone, bole ni, the water is dust!"
